UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 29, Lesson 1: Celebrations in our community', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion, harvest, history or friendship. Examples include Lunar New Year, Diwali, Eid, Christmas, Harmony Day and local shows. Celebrations help people share traditions and learn about each other.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort each into culture, religion or local community: Diwali; Christmas; the town agricultural show; Harmony Day.\n\nFEEDBACK\nDiwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural. The show is local community. Harmony Day celebrates cultural diversity.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose one celebration held in your community. Make a profile with what, when, who, how it is celebrated and why it matters. Include one respectful way for visitors to join. Use a local or family source.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion, harvest, history or friendship. Examples include Lunar New Year, Diwali, Eid, Christmas, Harmony Day and local shows. Celebrations help people share traditions and learn about each other.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose one celebration held in your community. Make a profile with what, when, who, how it is celebrated and why it matters. Include one respectful way for visitors to join. Use a local or family source. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using celebration on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community.. Correction: Diwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating celebration on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with celebration, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about celebration with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for celebration. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for celebration. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Diwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural"],"question":"Sort each into culture, religion or local community: Diwali; Christmas; the town agricultural show; Harmony Day. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for celebration; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community","Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion, harvest, history or friendship","Diwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural","Careful use of culture and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with celebration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named culture and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply celebration to new material and name the culture and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community","Name culture and explain why it matters only, without linking it to celebration"],"question":"Which response best applies celebration with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on celebration. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community","Diwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which response best uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence for celebration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community","Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about celebration","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about celebration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Example: Harmony Day on 21 March celebrates cultural diversity","Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about celebration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for celebration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name culture and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Choose one celebration held in your community","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community"],"question":"Which independent response best applies celebration to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about celebration with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating celebration on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using celebration on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for celebration. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with celebration, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community.. Correction: Diwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w29-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise celebration.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use celebration with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify culture and explain why it matters.","I can practise tradition independently on a new prompt.","I can notice diversity needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion, harvest, history or friendship.","options":["Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion, harvest, history or friendship","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about celebration with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Harmony Day on 21 March celebrates cultural diversity. A class records the day, why it is held and how people join in, such as wearing orange and sharing stories. Another entry lists a local community festival and who organises it.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort each into culture, religion or local community: Diwali; Christmas; the town agricultural show; Harmony Day.\n\nFEEDBACK\nDiwali and Christmas are religious celebrations for many people and also cultural. The show is local community. Harmony Day celebrates cultural diversity.","explain":"Communities celebrate for many reasons, such as culture, religion, harvest, history or friendship. Examples include Lunar New Year, Diwali, Eid, Christmas, Harmony Day and local shows. Celebrations help people share traditions and learn about each other.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing everyone in a country celebrates the same things ignores the many cultures within a community. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Two community celebrations.","caption":"Date, host and meaning.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Harmony Day</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">21 March, diversity</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Local show</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">community festival</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: Harmony Day on 21 March celebrates cultural diversity. A class records the day, why it is held and how people j Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk throug